From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bigjakk <6015188+bigjakk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: kcc: frame cap: start the client frame at the ack release (late input latch)

Feature: KccFrameCapLateLatch (default off), param slack_pct (default 25,
clamped to 0..90). All platforms; it only acts on
BackToBackBeginFrameSource, i.e. with --disable-frame-rate-limit, and only
while a frame cap limits the frame rate with room to spare.

What the stock code does (with ws-priority, frame-pacing and frame-cap
applied; one renderer, cap period P, renderer turnaround r << P, one
pending swap as on DirectComposition with two buffers)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
At each grid time T_k DisplayScheduler::RunDelayedSwapBuffersAck() ->
DidReceiveSwapBuffersAckImpl() -> BeginFrameSource::SetIsGpuBusy(false) ->
BackToBackBeginFrameSource::OnGpuNoLongerBusy() -> OnTimerTick(), which in
one task
  1. sends the renderer its next BeginFrame
     (CompositorFrameSinkSupport::OnBeginFrame), and
  2. through IssueBeginFrameToSchedulerClient() ->
     DisplayScheduler::OnBeginFrame() -> OnBeginFrameDeadline() draws and
     swaps the frame the renderer submitted at T_(k-1) + r. That draw also
     sends the renderer its DidReceiveCompositorFrameAck
     (DisplayDamageTracker::RunDrawCallbacks) and sets gpu-busy again.
The renderer gets BeginFrame then ack, and
SchedulerStateMachine::ShouldSendBeginMainFrame() turns true as soon as
the ack clears IsDrawThrottled(): BeginMainFrame (input dispatch, rAF)
starts at about T_k + 0.1 ms. The frame is submitted at T_k + r and then
sits in viz: DisplayScheduler::DesiredBeginFrameDeadlineMode() says "Swap
throttled" until the next release, where it is drawn at T_(k+1).

So the frame presented at T_(k+1) sampled its input at T_k: one full cap
period earlier (4.2 ms at 240, 6.9 ms at 144), whatever r is.

The survey named the just_submitted_in_deadline exception in
ShouldSendBeginMainFrame() as the cause. It is not involved:
did_send_begin_main_frame_for_current_frame_ prevents a second
BeginMainFrame in the impl frame that submitted, and the next BeginFrame
does wait for the release. The wait is simply spent after rendering
instead of before it. What keeps it that way is in viz:
BeginFrameSource::RequestCallbackOnGpuAvailable() lets one BeginFrame
through after the display has swapped (kOneBeginFrameAfterBusySent), and a
client that submits before the display has swapped gets a BeginFrame
straight away; either gives the client a head start of one frame, and once
a frame is queued behind the held ack the cycle above repeats forever.

What this patch changes
-----------------------
While the latch is engaged (see 3):
1. components/viz/common/frame_sinks/begin_frame_source.cc,
   RequestCallbackOnGpuAvailable(): no BeginFrame at all while the gpu is
   busy; the state goes from kIdle straight to kThrottled. The next
   BeginFrame is the one OnGpuNoLongerBusy() sends at the ack release.
2. components/viz/service/frame_sinks/compositor_frame_sink_support.cc,
   OnBeginFrame(): a sink that still has a submitted frame the display has
   not drawn (pending_frames_ > 0) skips the BeginFrame and does not call
   DidFinishFrame(), so the source sends it nothing more;
   DidReceiveCompositorFrameAck() calls DidFinishFrame() when that frame
   is drawn. This empties the one-frame queue of the stock order and
   closes the "submitted before the swap" path. It applies only when the
   sink is the only frame sink observing the source
   (BackToBackBeginFrameSource::ShouldHoldBeginFrameUntilFrameAck()), and
   never to the root sink, a bundled sink, a sink with a LayerContext, a
   sink with surfaces pending activation or one whose embedder is blocked
   on it.
3. components/viz/service/display/display_scheduler.cc decides when the
   latch is engaged. A client that starts at the release cannot overlap
   its frame with the previous swap any more, so it must fit r plus the
   swap-to-ack time into P. MaybeEngageFrameCapLateLatch() runs when a
   held ack is released in the stock order and measures exactly that:
   (time the ack has waited) + (time the queued frame has waited) - P is
   the time the ack would be held without pipelining. The latch engages
   when that is at least slack_pct of P. DidReceiveSwapBuffersAck()
   disengages it at the first ack that arrives after its grid slot;
   SetFrameCapInterval() and the destructor disengage it too.

Resulting order: ack release at T_k -> BeginFrame -> BeginMainFrame at
T_k + ~0.1 ms -> submit at T_k + r -> drawn at once (no swap pending) ->
nothing more until T_(k+1). Input is r old at the swap instead of P.

Trade-offs
----------
* Pacing. Stock presents sit on the release grid, because a finished frame
  is always waiting. With the latch they land at T_k + r + draw time, so
  the present-to-present interval now carries the frame-to-frame variation
  of r. Mean FPS is unchanged: the grid still paces the releases.
* A frame that takes longer than P is late by its overrun instead of being
  hidden by the queue. Its ack then arrives after its slot, the latch
  drops out, and the stock pipelining is back until a release shows enough
  slack again. Dropping out and re-engaging each cost one long interval
  (about P + r), so a client close to the cap should stay in the stock
  order; that is what slack_pct is for. A cap the machine cannot reach, or
  can only reach by pipelining, never engages the latch.
* Several frame sinks animating in one window (a second WebContentsView,
  the browser UI, a video, a capture): the hold in (2) is off, every sink
  starts at each release and is drawn at the next, as in the stock code.
  Only the single-sink case gets the late latch.
* The root (browser UI) sink can no longer get one BeginFrame during the
  hold; its next frame starts at the release, at most P later.
* With CustomMaxPendingFrames:count/2 the renderer could start a frame
  without waiting for its ack; viz now withholds the BeginFrame, so the
  latch works the same. Uncapped, nothing is touched: no ack is held, so
  the latch never engages.

No new waits are introduced: (1) is released by the same swap-ack ledger
that already releases the gpu-busy latch (timer, FlushPendingSwapAckDelay
on resize and forced swaps, SetFrameCapInterval, ~DisplayScheduler), and
(2) by the frame ack that every submitted frame gets.

How to A/B
----------
--enable-features=KccFrameCapLateLatch with a cap well below what the
machine can render (CustomFrameCap:fps/144 or win.setFrameCap(144)).
* In a trace with viz,cc,benchmark: time from "Scheduler::BeginImplFrame"
  (or the BeginMainFrame) in the renderer to the
  "Graphics.Pipeline.DrawAndSwap" that shows that frame. Expect about P
  without the flag and about r with it.
* "SendBeginFrameDecision" events with reason HoldUntilFrameAck should be
  rare in steady state (they mark a queue being drained).
* End to end: click-to-photon or mouse-to-photon with a latency tester;
  expect a drop of about P - r.
* PresentMon msBetweenPresents: mean unchanged, spread wider by the
  variation of r.
* Regression checks: resize the window while capped, hide/show it, change
  the cap at runtime (also to 0 and back), open a second view that
  animates, and set caps at and above what the machine reaches: FPS must
  not be lower than with the flag off. If it is, raise slack_pct.

Not verified (nothing was compiled or run)
------------------------------------------
* The timeline was read from source, not traced. In particular the task
  order on the viz thread at frame arrival (deadline task before tick) is
  inferred from both being immediate tasks posted in that order.
* The slack measurement and the 25% default are untested.
* macOS has two pending swaps (three buffers). The latch should work the
  same way there (gpu-busy is reached at two outstanding swaps), but the
  slack measurement uses the newest held ack and may be too pessimistic to
  ever engage it.
* --disable-features=DisplaySchedulerAsClient was checked by reading only.
  There the DisplayScheduler draws from inside its own observer callback,
  so while the latch is engaged BackToBackBeginFrameSource::OnTimerTick()
  calls that observer after the frame sinks, as the default mode does
  through IssueBeginFrameToSchedulerClient(). Otherwise a sink could be
  asked only after the display had drawn (and acked) its queued frame, and
  the queue would never be emptied.

